1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,200
It's morning in Borneo, and you
can't see the end of your own street.

2
00:00:04,200 --> 00:00:06,960
The sky is orange, and every
breath tastes like smoke.

3
00:00:08,067 --> 00:00:12,862
Between January and October 2026, fires
burned nearly two point nine million

4
00:00:12,862 --> 00:00:16,538
hectares of Indonesia, according
to one satellite monitor.

5
00:00:16,538 --> 00:00:18,905
That is close to the size of Belgium.

6
00:00:20,767 --> 00:00:21,847
So who lit the match?

7
00:00:22,787 --> 00:00:26,127
The honest answer is that in many
places, nobody has proven it yet.

8
00:00:27,467 --> 00:00:31,167
The sharper questions are who made
the land ready to burn and who pays?

9
00:00:32,247 --> 00:00:35,567
Meet the players, people like you,
companies holding concessions,

10
00:00:35,887 --> 00:00:37,147
and the state that grants them.

11
00:00:38,260 --> 00:00:42,016
A concession is a block of land
the state licenses to a company,

12
00:00:42,016 --> 00:00:44,904
for example to grow oil
palm, or trees for paper.

13
00:00:44,904 --> 00:00:48,600
Whoever holds that licence is
expected to keep it from burning.

14
00:00:49,700 --> 00:00:51,884
Now, two numbers that often get mixed up.

15
00:00:51,884 --> 00:00:55,472
Government data quoted by the press
showed about two hundred and two

16
00:00:55,472 --> 00:00:57,500
thousand hectares burned through July.

17
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:01,500
The satellite monitor counted nearly
two point nine million through October.

18
00:01:02,633 --> 00:01:06,453
Different months and different methods,
so you cannot set them head-to-head.

19
00:01:07,273 --> 00:01:09,233
Both still point to a very bad year.

20
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:13,880
To see why these fires are so
stubborn, look under your feet.

21
00:01:14,720 --> 00:01:16,940
Much of the danger sits
in a soil called peat.

22
00:01:18,100 --> 00:01:21,780
Peat is half-rotted plants piled up
in water over thousands of years.

23
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:25,780
Kept wet, it behaves like a soaked
sponge that is hard to light.

24
00:01:26,900 --> 00:01:27,780
Then come the ditches.

25
00:01:28,660 --> 00:01:32,300
Canals are dug to pull the water out
so crops like oil palm or paper trees

26
00:01:32,340 --> 00:01:32,900
can grow there.

27
00:01:34,033 --> 00:01:35,733
The sponge dries from the top down.

28
00:01:36,633 --> 00:01:39,553
What once resisted fire turns
into a deep store of fuel.

29
00:01:40,680 --> 00:01:45,372
A twenty twenty-five study of Sumatra
and Borneo compared peatland with and

30
00:01:45,372 --> 00:01:46,360
without canals.

31
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:50,744
Drained peat showed up to thirteen
times more fire hotspots per square

32
00:01:50,744 --> 00:01:51,420
kilometre.

33
00:01:52,547 --> 00:01:54,488
A peat fire can creep underground.

34
00:01:54,488 --> 00:01:57,372
It can smoulder out of
sight, sometimes for months,

35
00:01:57,372 --> 00:01:59,145
and it is very hard to put out.

36
00:02:00,913 --> 00:02:03,410
And the smoke ends up in people's chests.

37
00:02:03,410 --> 00:02:07,748
In Jambi province alone, health
officials recorded more than seventy-six

38
00:02:07,748 --> 00:02:11,671
thousand respiratory infections
from early August to mid October.

39
00:02:12,820 --> 00:02:14,625
The haze also crosses borders.

40
00:02:14,625 --> 00:02:19,053
It reached Malaysia and Singapore, and
Singapore's environment agency asked

41
00:02:19,053 --> 00:02:20,859
ten companies for information.

42
00:02:21,967 --> 00:02:23,567
So why would fire be used at all?

43
00:02:24,367 --> 00:02:27,647
One reason is money, because burning
is a cheap way to clear land.

44
00:02:28,767 --> 00:02:33,607
Sawit Watch, a palm oil watchdog
group, estimates fire can cut clearing

45
00:02:33,707 --> 00:02:36,247
costs by up to half
compared with machines.

46
00:02:37,167 --> 00:02:39,687
That is their estimate,
not an audited figure.

47
00:02:40,793 --> 00:02:43,369
On paper, clearing land
by burning is banned.

48
00:02:43,369 --> 00:02:46,113
A two thousand and nine
environment law says so.

49
00:02:47,233 --> 00:02:49,053
There is an exception for local custom.

50
00:02:49,673 --> 00:02:53,293
A family may burn up to two hectares
for local crop varieties with a

51
00:02:53,353 --> 00:02:54,253
firebreak around it.

52
00:02:56,133 --> 00:02:58,085
So whose land burned this year?

53
00:02:58,085 --> 00:03:02,721
On November thirteenth, a study pointed
to burned ground inside concessions

54
00:03:02,721 --> 00:03:04,429
tied to large listed firms.

55
00:03:05,553 --> 00:03:09,938
The Rainforest Action Network and
Nusantara Atlas found more than fourteen

56
00:03:09,938 --> 00:03:13,971
thousand six hundred burned hectares
in one pulpwood concession that

57
00:03:13,971 --> 00:03:15,724
supplies Asia Pulp and Paper.

58
00:03:15,724 --> 00:03:18,588
The paper company said
it was unable to comment.

59
00:03:19,733 --> 00:03:22,964
In a West Borneo palm concession
run by First Resources,

60
00:03:22,964 --> 00:03:26,761
they mapped at least three thousand
seven hundred burned hectares.

61
00:03:26,761 --> 00:03:29,650
The company says it follows
a zero-burning policy.

62
00:03:30,747 --> 00:03:35,052
In a Bumitama Agri concession, the
study counted four thousand four hundred

63
00:03:35,052 --> 00:03:38,677
and seventy-three hectares,
eighty-eight percent of it on peat.

64
00:03:38,677 --> 00:03:41,227
The company said it was
still investigating.

65
00:03:42,347 --> 00:03:43,565
Read this carefully.

66
00:03:43,565 --> 00:03:45,537
The study mapped where it burned.

67
00:03:45,537 --> 00:03:48,087
It did not establish
how the fires started.

68
00:03:49,247 --> 00:03:51,623
Alex Helan of the
Rainforest Action Network,

69
00:03:51,623 --> 00:03:53,997
one of the study's
authors, put it this way.

70
00:03:53,997 --> 00:03:58,060
The question is less who lit the match
than who drained the peat and created

71
00:03:58,060 --> 00:03:58,904
the conditions.

72
00:04:00,787 --> 00:04:03,105
There is also a serious counterpoint.

73
00:04:03,105 --> 00:04:07,497
The palm certification body, the
RSPO, cites data showing nearly ninety

74
00:04:07,497 --> 00:04:11,827
percent of land burned through
September lay outside palm concessions.

75
00:04:12,947 --> 00:04:16,167
Outside palm concessions can
mean forests, village land,

76
00:04:16,687 --> 00:04:19,047
or other kinds of
concession, such as pulpwood.

77
00:04:20,047 --> 00:04:22,827
It narrows one accusation
without answering all of them.

78
00:04:23,967 --> 00:04:28,069
The RSPO also says fire reached
about fourteen thousand five hundred

79
00:04:28,069 --> 00:04:32,050
hectares of certified concessions,
much of it in just two of them.

80
00:04:32,050 --> 00:04:36,210
It says it found no evidence that
any certified unit started fires on

81
00:04:36,210 --> 00:04:36,745
purpose.

82
00:04:37,880 --> 00:04:41,800
In Jambi, the environmental group
Walhi reported nine land holders to

83
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:45,832
police, and says they account for
more than a fifth of the burned area.

84
00:04:45,832 --> 00:04:50,032
One of them, a cooperative, says its
fire was neither deliberate nor meant

85
00:04:50,032 --> 00:04:50,872
to clear land.

86
00:04:51,967 --> 00:04:54,207
Police have moved on two fronts.

87
00:04:54,207 --> 00:04:58,687
In Jambi, by late October, all
sixteen suspects were individuals.

88
00:04:58,687 --> 00:05:03,167
Nationally, investigators had
named twenty companies as suspects.

89
00:05:04,280 --> 00:05:07,180
On November twenty-fourth
came number twenty-one,

90
00:05:07,180 --> 00:05:09,384
a palm company in Riau called PT MAS.

91
00:05:09,384 --> 00:05:13,850
Police allege a hundred and sixty
hectares burned inside its own concession,

92
00:05:13,850 --> 00:05:16,634
and that its early warning
system did not work.

93
00:05:17,747 --> 00:05:19,607
A suspect has not been found guilty.

94
00:05:20,067 --> 00:05:22,407
A court decides that,
and it can take years.

95
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:25,713
There are administrative penalties too.

96
00:05:25,713 --> 00:05:29,918
In September, the environment ministry
said it had sealed thirty companies,

97
00:05:29,918 --> 00:05:33,238
and in October the forestry
ministry sanctioned thirty-two.

98
00:05:35,100 --> 00:05:37,084
But does punishment actually land?

99
00:05:37,084 --> 00:05:41,447
As of twenty twenty-one, more than three
point four trillion rupiah in final

100
00:05:41,447 --> 00:05:45,358
court awards against fire-linked
firms had not reached the treasury.

101
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:50,408
From civil cases won between twenty
fifteen and twenty twenty-one,

102
00:05:50,408 --> 00:05:53,850
only about seventy-eight billion
rupiah had been executed.

103
00:05:53,850 --> 00:05:57,174
An enforcement official blamed
red tape, field problems,

104
00:05:57,174 --> 00:05:59,040
and the work of tracing assets.

105
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:03,448
Greenpeace looked at twenty
fifteen to twenty nineteen.

106
00:06:03,448 --> 00:06:07,856
By its count, eight of the ten palm
companies with the largest burned areas

107
00:06:07,856 --> 00:06:09,360
had received no sanction.

108
00:06:11,233 --> 00:06:15,111
Now, corruption, and here is
what a court has actually found.

109
00:06:15,111 --> 00:06:19,426
In twenty twenty-three, palm tycoon
Surya Darmadi was convicted over

110
00:06:19,426 --> 00:06:24,180
plantation permits he secured with a
district head back in two thousand and

111
00:06:24,180 --> 00:06:24,553
four.

112
00:06:25,667 --> 00:06:29,201
The Supreme Court later raised
the sentence to sixteen years.

113
00:06:29,201 --> 00:06:33,533
It also cut the money he must repay to
about two point two trillion rupiah,

114
00:06:33,533 --> 00:06:35,407
from roughly forty-two trillion.

115
00:06:36,553 --> 00:06:39,433
That case is about permits,
not about any fire this year.

116
00:06:39,433 --> 00:06:43,109
I found no court ruling that ties the
twenty twenty-six fires to bribery.

117
00:06:44,267 --> 00:06:48,701
What is on record is a past
corruption conviction over permits,

118
00:06:48,701 --> 00:06:53,066
old fines left uncollected, and,
in a count from 2015 to 2019,

119
00:06:53,066 --> 00:06:56,322
large burned concessions
that drew no penalty.

120
00:06:56,322 --> 00:06:59,647
That is enough reason to
keep asking questions.

121
00:07:00,733 --> 00:07:02,241
So who pays in the meantime?

122
00:07:02,241 --> 00:07:06,193
The World Bank estimated that the twenty
fifteen fires burned two point six

123
00:07:06,193 --> 00:07:09,365
million hectares and cost
Indonesia sixteen billion dollars.

124
00:07:10,467 --> 00:07:13,047
Bills like that land on
lungs, on closed schools,

125
00:07:13,127 --> 00:07:15,727
on grounded flights, and on lost harvests.

126
00:07:16,867 --> 00:07:21,766
For this year, a research group called
Celios estimates economic and health

127
00:07:21,766 --> 00:07:26,472
costs of roughly three hundred sixty
to five hundred sixty-five trillion

128
00:07:26,472 --> 00:07:26,988
rupiah.

129
00:07:26,988 --> 00:07:29,502
That is an estimate, not a final bill.

130
00:07:31,367 --> 00:07:32,487
So here is the scoreboard.

131
00:07:33,107 --> 00:07:37,107
Drained peat burns more easily, and
some of the burned ground sits inside

132
00:07:37,167 --> 00:07:38,147
company concessions.

133
00:07:39,047 --> 00:07:41,387
Who struck most of the
matches is still unproven.

134
00:07:41,847 --> 00:07:45,647
The new cases are still open, and old
fines were still waiting to be paid.

135
00:07:47,500 --> 00:07:48,640
So go back to the sponge.

136
00:07:49,100 --> 00:07:51,040
Keep it wet, and a match struggles.

137
00:07:51,560 --> 00:07:55,480
Drain it, and one spark from
anyone can burn for months.

138
00:07:57,367 --> 00:08:00,038
So watch more than the
hand holding the match.

139
00:08:00,038 --> 00:08:02,936
Watch who digs the canals,
whether fines get paid,

140
00:08:02,936 --> 00:08:06,347
and whether those twenty-one
company cases reach a verdict.

141
00:08:08,200 --> 00:08:11,300
If you have lived under this smoke,
tell me in the comments what you saw.

142
00:08:12,200 --> 00:08:14,100
Follow along to see where these cases end.
